MAX SALTONSTALL: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.920
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.840
So if you didn't see
Implementing Fintech

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.517
Security in the
Cloud, then stay tuned

00:00:08.517 --> 00:00:09.600
because here's the rewind.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.656 --> 00:00:18.780
Moving your company's
workloads to the public cloud

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.220
can be scary.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.821
It feels like a
huge security risk,

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:23.820
and often, you're dealing
with your most closely

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.920
guarded intellectual property.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.050
To succeed at migrating,
you need to figure out

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.614
how to protect your IP
from theft or attack,

00:00:30.614 --> 00:00:32.280
while also making
sure your stakeholders

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.260
can get access to
the data they need.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.250
Let's see how Two Sigma
did it, start to finish.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.800
Based in New York, Two Sigma
is a tech-driven investment

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.870
company that's
moving to the cloud,

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:46.560
while also juggling the needs of
internal modelers, developers,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.920
and security teams, alongside
external communities of users

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.620
and developers.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.300
We'll walk through how they
created a secure Google Cloud

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.930
Platform architecture, opening
up new opportunities for scale

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.750
that they could not achieve
with on-premise solutions.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.510
They begin, like any of
us, with a brand new GCP

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.496
account and a super
admin identity or two.

00:01:06.496 --> 00:01:08.620
Of course, they have existing
on premise resources,

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.740
including their authoritative
identity provider,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.960
in this case, Active Directory.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:16.920
Then, they create a few
compute and storage resources.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.550
Securing those cloud assets
requires good policies.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.490
In this case, we'll use identity
and access management policies

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.130
to restrict who can
access storage buckets,

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.440
compute instances, and
both front and back end

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.280
applications, even before we
create any substantive content.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.860
As we continue to build and
secure this architecture,

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.550
we repeatedly ask ourselves,
what have we secured

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.950
and what are the
existing concerns?

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:44.610
What's still vulnerable,
or what could be abused?

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.860
Where do we have opportunities
to improve our ability

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.030
to audit?

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:51.040
And how can we make things
easier for our stakeholders?

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.770
Let's take your
super admin accounts.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.740
These are very, very powerful
and need strong protections

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.190
because they could do almost
anything with your data, which

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.380
is why leveraging
security keys is

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.410
essential for these
high value accounts.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:05.670
Or maybe all accounts?

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:11.580
Security keys guard against
phishing more effectively

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.710
than alternate second factors.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.620
That's because you can't fool a
security key with a fake domain

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.210
or a fake certificate.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.910
We've seen tremendous benefits
at Google from using them,

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.010
and many clients utilize
this open standard

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:24.640
to safeguard their accounts too.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:26.310
Even when accounts
are very secure,

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.290
people can leak your
company's data accidentally.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.620
You can prevent this
with org-wide policies,

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.450
in this case,
preventing any resource

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.050
from be accessed by
anybody outside the domain.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.170
Going even further, we've
created a virtual private cloud

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.750
construct inside
our GCP architecture

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.150
so that no app
data inside the VPC

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.790
can be accessed from
anything outside.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.500
This creates a strong
network boundary

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.290
and prevents data from going
to another GCP project which

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:53.880
could leak it.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.730
Keeping your data safe also
requires preventing employees

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.590
from exfiltrating data.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.440
To prevent a malicious insider
from moving data from one GCP

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.350
resource to another, you
can restrict all resources

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:08.430
inside your VPC to be accessible
only to other resources

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:10.170
inside the same VPC.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.990
On top of this, you can
use Identity-Aware Proxy

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.900
to allow specific
people to access the VPC

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.330
resources from anywhere
on the internet,

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.720
especially useful for
contractors or remote workers

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:22.260
who need to connect
to these systems

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.030
from outside your
office environment.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.890
IAP restricts access
based on identity,

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:30.000
so you choose who gets access,
rather than getting access

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.839
based on where someone sits.

00:03:31.839 --> 00:03:33.630
Now that you've got a
great security setup,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.470
you're done, right?

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:39.920
If things do go wrong, and
they probably will sometime,

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.100
you'll need to find out
what happened and when.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.470
We'll use Stackdriver
to manage logs, tracking

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.020
what happened every
step of the way

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:49.460
so you can investigate
after an incident.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:51.740
To get a view of your
overall security status,

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.690
we'll use Cloud
Security Command Center

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:55.220
to aggregate your
security metrics

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.080
into a single dashboard.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.480
And we'll use Forseti to
increase your automation

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:02.480
around security policy
application and monitoring

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.410
so new projects will adhere to
the same security standards.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:08.570
Securing your GCP
resources relies

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.660
on layering policies and
good architecture practices

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:14.150
and is possible even if
you're starting from scratch,

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:15.920
as you've seen here.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.100
Thanks for watching.

